{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1740,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To find the prime factors of a 2048 number\nit would take a classical computer millions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7700,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of years, a quantum computer could do it in\njust minutes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12179,
    "dDurationMs": 3911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that is because a quantum computer is\nbuilt on qubits, these devices which take"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16090,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advantage of quantum super position to reduce\nthe number of steps required to complete the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20810,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22220,
    "dDurationMs": 4709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how do you actually make a qubit in practice\nand how do you read and write information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26929,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27929,
    "dDurationMs": 3781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I met up with researchers who are using the\nouter most electron in a phosphorous atom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31710,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a qubit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32870,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This single phosphorous atom is embedded in\na silicon crystal right next to a tiny transistor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38900,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the electron has a magnetic dipole called\nits spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42310,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it has two orientations, up or down, which\nare like the classical one and zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48210,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now to differentiate the energy state of the\nelectron when it is in spin up and spin down,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52990,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you need to apply a strong magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55630,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e And to do that we use a super conducting\nmagnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58270,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So super conducting magnet is a large solenoid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62040,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is a coil of super conducting wire that\nsits inside of that vessel that is full of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67170,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid helium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68170,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So now the electron will line up with its\nspin pointing down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71340,
    "dDurationMs": 1639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is its lowest energy state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72979,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it would take some energy to put it into\nthe spin up state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77049,
    "dDurationMs": 4081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually not that much energy and if it\nwere at room temperature the electron would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81130,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have so much thermal energy that it would\nbe bouncing around from spin up to spin down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85990,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and back."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86990,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so you need to cool down the whole apparatus\nto only a few hundredths of a degree above"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91159,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absolute zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92799,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That way you know that the electron will definitely\nbe spin down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96539,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e There is not enough thermal energy in the\nsurroundings to flip it the other way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101380,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Now if you want to write information onto\nthe qubit, you can put the electron into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105159,
    "dDurationMs": 3411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin up state by hitting it with a pulse of\nmicrowaves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108570,
    "dDurationMs": 5579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that pulse needs to be a very specific\nfrequency and that frequency depends on the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114149,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic field that the electron is sitting\nin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116659,
    "dDurationMs": 5441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So what you see here is the frequency that\nis being produced by this microwave source"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122100,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it is 45.021 gigahertz, which in the magnetic\nfield that we are applying now is the resonance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129500,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency of the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131240,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So the electron is a little bit like a\nradio that can only tune in to one station."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135940,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And when that station is broadcasting, the\nelectron gets all excited and turns to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139550,
    "dDurationMs": 1049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin up state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140599,
    "dDurationMs": 1821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e But you can stop at any point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142420,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you just make a new tape and stop your\npulse and some specific point, what you have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147599,
    "dDurationMs": 5821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "created is a special quantum super position\nof the spin up and spin down states with a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153420,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specific phase between the two super positions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156450,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e And how do you read out the information?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157880,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, you use the transistor that this phosphorous\natom is embedded next to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162420,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e The spin down has the lower energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164720,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the spin up has the higher energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167240,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now in this transistor there is, in fact,\na little bundle of electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173570,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This bundle of electrons is filled up up to\na certain axis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178110,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This vertical axis here is energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179910,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And here we have got all these electrons that\nline up in energy just like the electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185280,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the shells of an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186980,
    "dDurationMs": 6141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now if the electron is pointing up, it\ncan jump into the transistor, right, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193121,
    "dDurationMs": 1909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it has more energy than all the others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195030,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It leaves behind the bare nuclear charge of\nthe phosphorous, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200870,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The phosphorous one more positive charge in\nthe nucleus as compared to silicon, but normally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206160,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is neutralized by the extra electron so\nthe two things cancel out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209730,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you take the electron away, then the\nphosphorous has a positive charge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213920,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it is as if you have a positive voltage,\na more positive voltage applied to this gate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219580,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t come from the gate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220600,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It comes from the atom, but is the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222100,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is just a positive voltage."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223580,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e It is like the transistor has been switched\nmore on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227010,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And so you see a pulse of current and that\nindicates that the electron was in the spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231110,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232110,
    "dDurationMs": 3951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e In this measurement phase, if you find\none of these spikes of current, it is because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236061,
    "dDurationMs": 1369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you had an electron spin up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237430,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it can play catching a spin up or a spin\ndown event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241330,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You use, there was no current here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242780,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was a spin down event."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244050,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And try again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245840,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again a spin down electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249120,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spin up electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250210,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Now these researchers have actually gone\nfurther using the nucleus of the phosphorous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254320,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom as a qubit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255820,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like an electron, the nucleus has a spin,\nalthough it is 2000 times weaker than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260979,
    "dDurationMs": 1291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin of the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262270,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you can still write to it the same way\nusing electromagnetic radiation, only it needs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267000,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be a longer wavelength and a longer pulse\nin order to get the spin to flip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272539,
    "dDurationMs": 6641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Because it is so small, so weakly magnetic\nand so perfectly isolated from the rest of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279180,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world, it is a qubit that lives for a\nvery long time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283289,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e But how do you read out the spin of the\nnucleus?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286229,
    "dDurationMs": 2571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, you use the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288800,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that the electron’s frequency that\nit will respond to depends on the magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293409,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field that it is sitting in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294409,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So that magnetic field is the external\nmagnetic field that is produced by the super"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299159,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computing magnet, but there is also an internal\nmagnetic field coming from the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304139,
    "dDurationMs": 2791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that internal magnetic field can have\ntwo directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306930,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307930,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The nucleus can be pointing up or down itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309580,
    "dDurationMs": 7780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what it means is that there are two frequencies\nat which the electron can respond, depending"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317360,
    "dDurationMs": 2309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the direction of the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319669,
    "dDurationMs": 3381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So the nucleus actually acts as a little\nselector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323050,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells the electron, basically, which radio\nstation it can listen to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327740,
    "dDurationMs": 7090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So what you are looking at now is an experiment\nwhere we actually flip the nucleus every five"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334830,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336020,
    "dDurationMs": 5669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So for five seconds you will see that the\nelectron always responds, because the nucleus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341689,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is always in the right direction to make the\nelectron respond to the frequency we are applying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346249,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347449,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then for the other five seconds, the electron\nwill not respond, because we have flipped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351229,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nucleus the other way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352789,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now watch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353789,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You see?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354789,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So in this period of time the nucleus has\nbeen flipped down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358079,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Yeah."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359079,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e And now after five seconds it will flip\nup and then..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361219,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Yeah, you see?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362219,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e And then the electron starts responding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363479,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Yeah."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364479,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you are watching on the oscilloscope screen\nin real time the measurement of the direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370139,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a single nucleus and our ability to flip\nit at will every five seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374889,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e The spin of the single nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376479,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377479,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e Now because all of this depends so sensitively\non magnetic fields, you need to make sure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381449,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to eliminate all spin from the silicon crystal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384629,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, unfortunately, natural silicon contains\nabout five percent the isotope silicon 29"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390939,
    "dDurationMs": 1211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that does have a spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392150,
    "dDurationMs": 5769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e But, in fact, the beauty of silicon is\nthat it has this isotope called silicon 28"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397919,
    "dDurationMs": 1541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that has no nuclear spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399460,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The nuclear spin is zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401220,
    "dDurationMs": 2419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it is a completely non magnetic atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403639,
    "dDurationMs": 3721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e But where are you going to find a pure\ncrystal of silicon 28?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407360,
    "dDurationMs": 2089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, wait."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409449,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e These isotopically purified silicon 28\ncrystals are being produced anyway for a purpose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414819,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "completely different from particle computing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416599,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are being produced to redefine the kilogram\nthrough the Avogadro project."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420719,
    "dDurationMs": 5771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\u003e\u003e So the off cuts from that silicon sphere\nare actually being used as the home for qubits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426490,
    "dDurationMs": 2829,
    "segs": [ {
